{"rows":[{"n":1,"file":"topics/01-java-core-jvm.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":2,"file":"topics/02-java-8-25-java-vs-go.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":3,"file":"topics/03-spring-boot-deep-build.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":4,"file":"topics/04-rest-grpc-webflux.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":5,"file":"topics/05-db-core-index-lock.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":6,"file":"topics/06-db-scaling.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":7,"file":"topics/07-sql-nosql-db-engines.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":9,"file":"topics/09-distributed-tx-fintech.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":12,"file":"topics/12-architecture-patterns.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":13,"file":"topics/13-security-oauth2.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":14,"file":"topics/14-devops-k8s-best-practices.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":15,"file":"topics/15-network-i-o-models.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":16,"file":"topics/16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.json","topic_type":"algorithm","system_design_items":["16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q1","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q2","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q3","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q4","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.presenting-dissecting-the-ota-project.q5","16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.whiteboard-dsa.q1"]},{"n":17,"file":"topics/17-rest-api-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":18,"file":"topics/18-query-optimization.json","topic_type":"data"},{"n":19,"file":"topics/19-dsa-leetcode.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":20,"file":"topics/20-observability-sre.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":21,"file":"topics/21-linux-production-debug.json","topic_type":"platform"},{"n":22,"file":"topics/22-low-level-design-ood.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":23,"file":"topics/23-java-concurrency-coding.json","topic_type":"algorithm"},{"n":24,"file":"topics/24-domain-driven-design.json","topic_type":"design"},{"n":25,"topic_type":"microservice","file":"topics/25-microservice.json"},{"n":26,"file":"topics/26-testing-strategy.json","topic_type":"core"},{"n":28,"file":"topics/28-distributed-lock-lease.json","topic_type":"design"}],"row":{"n":8,"file":"topics/08-message-queue.json","topic_type":"data"},"meta":{"topic_type":"data","key":"08-message-queue","vi":{"label":"Message queue","title":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","intro":"Xương sống của hệ event-driven. Tận dụng nền RabbitMQ Publisher Confirms của bạn, và mở rộng sang Kafka để nắm cả hai trường phái: broker queue vs log phân tán.","tags":["RabbitMQ exchange","Kafka partition","Delivery semantics","Publisher confirms","DLQ","Exactly-once","Event sourcing","Consumer tuning","Schema Registry"]},"en":{"label":"Message queue","title":"Message queue — RabbitMQ vs Kafka","intro":"The backbone of an event-driven system. Builds on your RabbitMQ Publisher Confirms background and extends into Kafka, so you hold both schools: broker queue versus distributed log.","tags":["RabbitMQ exchange","Kafka partition","Delivery semantics","Publisher confirms","DLQ","Exactly-once","Event sourcing","Consumer tuning","Schema Registry"]},"created_at":"2026-07-31","updated_at":"2026-10-26"},"topicMeta":{"1":{"en":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"},"vi":{"label":"Java Core & JVM"}},"2":{"en":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"},"vi":{"label":"Java 8→25 & Java vs Go"}},"3":{"en":{"label":"Spring Boot deep dive + build"},"vi":{"label":"Spring Boot deep + build"}},"4":{"en":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"},"vi":{"label":"REST/gRPC & WebFlux"}},"5":{"en":{"label":"DB core: indexes & locking"},"vi":{"label":"DB core: index & lock"}},"6":{"en":{"label":"DB scaling"},"vi":{"label":"DB scaling"}},"7":{"en":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"},"vi":{"label":"SQL/NoSQL & DB engines"}},"8":{"en":{"label":"Message queue"},"vi":{"label":"Message queue"}},"9":{"en":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed tx (fintech)"}},"10":{"en":{"label":"System design + rate limit"},"vi":{"label":"System design + rate limit"}},"11":{"en":{"label":"System design cases"},"vi":{"label":"System design cases"}},"12":{"en":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"},"vi":{"label":"Architecture & patterns"}},"13":{"en":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"},"vi":{"label":"Security & OAuth2"}},"14":{"en":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"},"vi":{"label":"DevOps · K8s · best practices"}},"15":{"en":{"label":"Network & I/O models"},"vi":{"label":"Network & I/O models"}},"16":{"en":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"},"vi":{"label":"Project · concurrency · whiteboard"}},"17":{"en":{"label":"REST API design"},"vi":{"label":"Thiết kế REST API"}},"18":{"en":{"label":"Query optimisation"},"vi":{"label":"Tối ưu query"}},"19":{"en":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"},"vi":{"label":"DSA & LeetCode"}},"20":{"en":{"label":"Observability & SRE"},"vi":{"label":"Observability & SRE"}},"21":{"en":{"label":"Linux & production debug"},"vi":{"label":"Linux & production debug"}},"22":{"en":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"},"vi":{"label":"Low-level design / OOD"}},"23":{"en":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"},"vi":{"label":"Java concurrency coding"}},"24":{"en":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"},"vi":{"label":"Domain-Driven Design"}},"25":{"en":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"},"vi":{"label":"Microservices at Scale — Technical Mastery Track"}},"26":{"en":{"label":"Testing"},"vi":{"label":"Testing"}},"27":{"en":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"},"vi":{"label":"API Gateway & Identity Edge"}},"28":{"en":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"},"vi":{"label":"Distributed Lock & Lease"}}},"reviewedAt":{"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1":"2026-08-01","08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q2":"2026-08-23","08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q3":"2026-08-01","08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q4":"2026-08-01","08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q9":"2026-10-25","08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q10":"2026-08-10","08-message-queue.the-two-models.q4":"2026-08-22","08-message-queue.the-two-models.q3":"2026-10-25","08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q5":"2026-10-26"},"lang":"en","body":{"sections":[{"title":"The two models","items":[{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q1","q":"The RabbitMQ model: exchange, queue, binding, routing key?","a":"A producer publishes to an exchange. The exchange routes the message to one or more queues through a binding and a routing key.\n\n**Exchange types:**\n- direct: exact routing-key match\n- topic: wildcard routing, such as booking.*.created\n- fanout: broadcast to every bound queue\n- headers: route by message headers\n\nRabbitMQ is sometimes summarized as a smart broker, dumb consumer. Treat that as shorthand only: the broker owns routing and delivery state, while the consumer still owns validation, authorization, deduplication, retries and business effects.\n\n**Durability is a chain, not one switch.** Every link must match the failure domain you designed for:\n- durable topology and persistent-message intent\n- queue type and quorum/replica placement\n- storage and publisher-confirm policy\n\nA quorum queue can improve survival of a defined node failure. It does not guarantee survival of correlated storage loss, deletion, expiration or every acknowledgement ambiguity.\n\nPublisher confirm means the broker accepted the publish under its configured contract. A consumer ACK only advances the broker's delivery boundary; neither proves that a database write, payment or other business effect completed.\n\nBound in-flight work with prefetch: a larger value can improve batching, but can also increase memory, unfairness and redelivery cost.\n\nFor the full delivery vocabulary, use the four-boundary model in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1).","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q2","q":"The Kafka model: topic, partition, offset, consumer group?","a":"Kafka is a retained distributed log:\n- a topic is split into partitions\n- each record has an offset within its partition\n- consumers in the same consumer group share partition ownership, so at most one active member normally processes a partition at a time\n\nRecords remain available according to retention and compaction policy rather than disappearing simply because a consumer read them. Different groups can consume independently and replay by choosing offsets within the retained window.\n\nAn offset is a consumer cursor, not proof that the business effect committed. Commit after the protected effect for an at-least-once flow; committing first trades duplicates for possible loss.\n\nA key can keep related records in one partition, but it does not create global order, and adding or migrating partitions requires an explicit ordering/version strategy.\n\n**Each guarantee covers one boundary:**\n- producer acknowledgement or a Kafka transaction covers the producer/broker or Kafka-internal boundary\n- replication and retention decide whether replay is still available\n- the consumer's offset commit and its business side effect are two more, separate boundaries\n\nKafka transactions can provide exactly-once read-process-write behavior inside their supported Kafka scope, not an arbitrary database or external provider.","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q3","q":"RabbitMQ vs Kafka — when do you use which?","a":"<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>RabbitMQ</th><th>Kafka</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Primary abstraction</td><td>exchange → queue → delivery</td><td>topic → partition → retained log</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Routing</td><td>exchange and binding rules</td><td>producer partitioning, usually by key</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Work ownership</td><td>competing consumers and ACK/requeue</td><td>consumer groups and committed offsets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Replay</td><td>queue/DLX/retention design</td><td>retained records and independent offsets</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ordering scope</td><td>queue/delivery lane, affected by redelivery and concurrency</td><td>one partition, affected by key, retries and handlers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Throughput</td><td colspan=\"2\">workload-dependent; measure payload, batching, replication, consumers and retention</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n:::tip Bottom line\nChoose from the workload contract. RabbitMQ is often a good fit for routed commands and discrete tasks; Kafka is often a good fit for retained event streams, replay, fan-out and partition-scoped order.\n\nA system may use both, but each flow must name its owner, retention, failure domain and recovery path.\n:::\n\n**Kafka 4.2 narrowed the queue gap.** Share groups (KIP-932, production-ready in 4.2) let consumers take records from the same partition without exclusive assignment, with per-record acknowledgement — `ACCEPT`, `RELEASE`, `REJECT` — and a delivery-count limit.\n\nThey trade away ordering: use them for work processed one record at a time, not for a stream whose order matters.\n\nBefore Kafka 4.4 added dead-letter queues for share groups (KIP-1191), a record past the limit was archived rather than copied anywhere — so on 4.2 and 4.3 poison handling still needs its own path.\n\nA broker choice does not choose delivery semantics for you. Publisher confirmation, broker retention/replication, consumer ACK or offset, and the business effect remain distinct. See (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1) before making a durability or exactly-once claim.","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.the-two-models.q4","q":"Message queue vs pub/sub: how do delivery ownership and failure isolation differ?","a":"The decision is about delivery ownership, not the transport brand:\n\n<table class='cmp'><thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Who owns a delivery?</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Queue / competing consumers</td><td>One active consumer in the group handles a task</td><td>One command or unit of work</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pub/sub</td><td>Each subscription or consumer group owns its reaction</td><td>One fact, many independent reactions</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nIn RabbitMQ, one queue with multiple consumers is queue-like; a fanout exchange with a queue per subscriber is pub/sub-like. In Kafka, consumers in the same group share work, while different groups have independent cursors.\n\nGive each independent subscriber its own queue or group so one subscriber's lag, retry policy or poison message does not block another. Bound in-flight work, retry with a defined policy, and use a dead-letter/quarantine path where appropriate.\n\n**The shape still does not promise at-most-once or exactly-once.** Each signal proves one thing:\n- a publisher confirm means broker acceptance\n- retention and replication determine whether recovery is possible\n- an ACK or offset records a consumer decision\n- only the consumer's durable business transaction proves its protected effect\n\nUse a stable event identity and a versioned contract so duplicates and permitted reordering are safe.","difficulty":"core"}]},{"title":"Reliability & delivery semantics","items":[{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q1","q":"What do the delivery semantics at-most-once / at-least-once / exactly-once mean?","a":"Delivery semantics describe what may happen to one logical delivery within a named boundary. They do not, by themselves, guarantee disk durability, infinite retention, producer correctness or an external business effect.\n\nUse four boundaries:\n\n1. **Producer → broker acceptance:** did the broker accept the publication? Examples are a RabbitMQ publisher confirm or a Kafka producer acknowledgement.\n2. **Broker retention/commit:** can the record be recovered after a consumer crash? Examples are queue durability/replication, Kafka retention and offsets, or a provider's visibility/retention policy.\n3. **Broker → consumer delivery:** did the consumer acknowledge or commit its cursor? A RabbitMQ ACK, Kafka offset or visibility completion is this boundary.\n4. **Consumer → business effect:** did the protected database state or external effect commit? This needs a local transaction, inbox/business key, provider idempotency or reconciliation as appropriate.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th>Semantic</th><th>Typical protocol behavior</th><th>Application consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>at-most-once</td><td>acknowledge/commit before processing or do not retry</td><td>a failure can lose work; duplicates are traded for loss</td></tr>\n<tr><td>at-least-once</td><td>redeliver until acknowledgement within availability and retention limits</td><td>the handler can run more than once; the effect needs deduplication or reconciliation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>exactly-once within a scope</td><td>atomically commit input position and output/state, or deduplicate under a defined protocol</td><td>no duplicate committed result inside that scope; arbitrary external systems remain outside it</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n**The crash window decides which failure you get:**\n- ACK before the effect: a crash loses the work.\n- Effect committed, crash before ACK: the message is redelivered, so the effect must be safe to repeat.\n\nKafka transactions can provide a scoped internal guarantee; RabbitMQ plus a database inbox can make a database effect converge, but the ACK and database transaction are still separate.\n\nFor the consumer-side pattern, see (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6) and (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2).\n\n:::deep\n**What would change this answer** — state these before defending the design; each is an assumption the interviewer can ask you to justify, and a measurement that would falsify it:\n\n- Which broker and version, and which defaults you pinned — retries, acks, prefetch, `min.insync.replicas` — because \"at-least-once\" is a property of a configuration, not of a product.\n- What the primary workload is: commands, durable domain events, replayable analytics, or an ordered per-aggregate stream. The right answer for one is wrong for another.\n- The loss you can tolerate, the cost of a duplicate, the oldest-message age you can live with and the replay window — the numbers that decide queue versus log.\n- Which external effects sit inside the guarantee. \"At-least-once plus idempotent consumer\" is falsified by any effect with no stable business identity, no durable dedup or reconciliation, or an irreversible side effect fired before the record commits.\n- Who owns the DLQ, how long it is retained, who may redrive it, and the maximum age before it is an incident.\n:::","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q2","q":"How do RabbitMQ Publisher Confirms and consumer ack/prefetch work?","a":"RabbitMQ exposes separate producer and consumer boundaries:\n\n- **Publisher confirms** report whether the broker accepted a publish according to the selected topology, queue type and durability/replication contract. A confirm is not a processing acknowledgement.\n- **Returns** with mandatory report that a message could not be routed. A message can be returned and still receive a positive publisher confirm, so handle both signals.\n- **Consumer ACKs** tell RabbitMQ that the consumer accepts responsibility for that delivery. An unacknowledged message may be redelivered; an ACK does not prove that an external effect was correct.\n- **Prefetch** bounds unacknowledged deliveries. Tune it from handler time, payload size, fairness and memory; raising it is not a substitute for capacity or backpressure.\n\nDurability is a chain: durable topology, persistent-message intent, queue replication, confirm policy, storage and the tested failure domain. A quorum acknowledgement does not promise survival of every correlated node/storage loss, operator action or expiration.\n\nChoose the RabbitMQ data structure for the workload: a quorum queue gives replicated queue semantics, while a stream is an append/replay shape for retained backlog or fan-out.\n\nA stream is not a drop-in replacement for task-queue acknowledgement, per-message work ownership, or the application's external-effect contract.\n\nCrash windows matter: a database effect committed before ACK can be delivered again, while ACK before the effect can lose work.\n\nCouple the consumer's deduplication/inbox record with its local effect where the flow is at-least-once; the broader idempotency contract belongs in (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q2).\n\nPublisher confirms also do not make a database write and publication atomic. Use the queue-specific Outbox boundary in (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3).","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q3","q":"How does Kafka guarantee durability: replication, ISR, acks?","a":"Kafka durability is defined per partition and acknowledged record:\n\n- a partition has a leader and replicas; the ISR is the set currently considered in sync\n- acks=0 does not wait, acks=1 waits for the leader, and acks=all waits for the current ISR under broker semantics\n- min.insync.replicas can reject an acks=all write when the ISR is too small instead of silently weakening the policy\n- replication factor controls placement count; rack/zone awareness, election policy and storage health shape the failure domain\n\nA policy such as replication factor 3, min.insync.replicas=2, acks=all and producer idempotence may be appropriate for a tested single-broker failure model.\n\nIt is not a universal no-loss recipe: correlated zone/storage loss, retention expiry, operator error and lost acknowledgement responses still need an explicit recovery contract.\n\nProducer idempotence can prevent duplicate log appends from supported retries, but it does not make a consumer's database or external call exactly once.\n\nCommitting an offset after an effect gives at-least-once behavior; committing before the effect risks loss. Kafka transactions can atomically join consumed offsets and produced Kafka records inside their supported Kafka boundary.\n\nMonitor ISR shrink, under-replicated/offline partitions, produce errors/latency, retention and consumer lag, then rehearse the broker/zone failure you claim to tolerate.","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q4","q":"Ordering: how do the two brokers guarantee it?","a":"Ordering must be scoped to a key and an observable effect; neither broker gives a free global order.\n\n<table class=\"cmp\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Kafka</th><th>RabbitMQ</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Broker order</td><td>append order within one partition</td><td>queue/delivery order, subject to queue features and redelivery</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Parallelism</td><td>different partitions progress independently</td><td>competing consumers and handlers run concurrently; single-active-consumer narrows the lane</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Application risk</td><td>key changes, partition migration, producer retries, rebalances and parallel handlers</td><td>requeue/redelivery and concurrent handlers can complete effects out of order</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nUse a stable aggregate key only when every event sharing the invariant uses the same partitioning contract.\n\n**Delivery order is not completion order:**\n- producer idempotence and supported in-flight settings can preserve partition order under retries, yet application handlers can still reorder effects\n- a RabbitMQ queue can narrow delivery order, but a crash/redelivery or asynchronous database work can still change completion order\n\nWhen stale or out-of-order state is harmful, carry an aggregate sequence/version and apply it with an atomic condition. Define gap, duplicate, replay and partition-migration handling.\n\nA global single partition sacrifices throughput and availability and still cannot order arbitrary external systems atomically.\n\nTest two producers for one key, retries, rebalance/failover, delayed old events, poison-message bypass and DLQ replay. The broader messaging concurrency card is (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q2).","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q5","q":"DLQ and poison messages — how are they handled?","a":"A poison message is one that keeps failing and can block useful work if it is retried forever. A DLQ or dead-letter topic is a quarantine state, not proof of reliability.\n\n- classify failures before retrying: transient dependency failure, throttling, schema/contract error, authorization error and invalid business input have different actions\n- use bounded attempts with exponential backoff and jitter; keep retry work out of the hot delivery path when it would block a partition or queue\n- after quarantine, record the original identity, attempt/error metadata and first-seen age; assign an owner, retention, alert threshold and redrive authorization\n- validate code/schema/configuration before replay, rate-limit the redrive, and make the consumer effect safe for duplicates or reconcile unknown outcomes\n\nRabbitMQ commonly uses retry queues/TTL and a dead-letter exchange; Kafka commonly uses retry topics and a dead-letter topic. These are topology choices, not universal semantics.\n\n**Quorum queues can now hold the retry themselves (as of 2026-10-26).** Since RabbitMQ 4.3, delayed retry (`delayed-retry-type`, `-min`, `-max` as a policy) holds a returned message for `min(min × delivery count, max)` before redelivery: linear back-off with no TTL queue.\n\nRabbitMQ 4.4 adds delayed delivery: a message whose `x-opt-delivery-time` is in the future is enqueued now and delivered at that time. Neither replaces the delivery limit (default 20 on quorum queues): past it the message is still dead-lettered, so the DLX is still needed.\n\nMonitor oldest quarantined age and recovery outcome, not only queue size.\n\nConsumer deduplication is covered in (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6); broader API/business idempotency belongs in (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1).","difficulty":"core"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6","q":"Idempotent consumer — how do you implement dedup?","a":"At-least-once delivery can run a handler more than once. Use a stable event or command identity, not a delivery tag or offset that changes when the message is redelivered.\n\nTwo common designs are:\n\n- make the business operation naturally idempotent, such as an UPSERT or an absolute-state update with a version condition\n- insert the event ID or business key into an Inbox/processed table with a unique constraint in the **same local transaction** as the protected business effect; a duplicate then becomes a no-op or an explicit conflict\n\nThe deduplication window must cover the retry, retention and approved replay window, or the old event can become new work again.\n\nThe broker can suppress some duplicate publications inside its own scope, but that does not make a database write, payment call or email exactly once.\n\n**Beyond the consumer:**\n- external provider idempotency: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q2)\n- timeout/unknown outcomes and reconciliation: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q1)\n- where eventual consistency is acceptable with money: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.correctness-where-money-is-involved.q6)\n- the idempotency-key contract: (25-microservice.09-idempotency-the-central-link.q1)\n\nThis topic owns the message-to-consumer boundary; keep complete payment, Saga and API idempotency workflows in their canonical topics.","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q7","q":"The Outbox pattern with a message queue — why is it needed?","a":"A database commit followed by a broker publish is a dual write. A crash between the two can leave durable business state with no publication, or a publication with no matching state.\n\nAn Outbox makes only the **local** boundary atomic:\n\n1. In one database transaction, commit the business state change and an integration-event intent in an Outbox table.\n2. A polling relay or CDC connector reads pending rows and publishes them.\n3. The relay records progress only after its own publication/confirmation policy; a crash can make the relay publish the same event again.\n4. Consumers therefore need stable event identity and an Inbox/idempotent effect.\n\nA publisher confirm helps establish broker acceptance for a relay attempt. It does not atomically include the relay's database bookkeeping, consumer ACK, downstream database, payment provider or other business effect. Do not describe Outbox plus confirm as end-to-end exactly once.\n\n**Where each part is explained in depth:**\n- outbox mechanics and the dual-write failure: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q3)\n- consumer deduplication: (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6)\n- Saga/compensation: (09-distributed-tx-fintech.distributed-transaction-patterns.q2)","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q8","q":"Event-driven architecture & event sourcing — concepts and trade-offs?","a":"Event-driven architecture communicates through asynchronous facts or commands. It can decouple deployables and absorb bursts, but it also spreads the flow across consumers, introduces lag and eventual consistency, and requires tracing, replay controls and failure ownership.\n\nEvent sourcing makes an append-only event history the authoritative state for a bounded aggregate; current state is rebuilt by replay, often with snapshots and separate read models.\n\nIt is not automatically a perfect audit trail: completeness, immutability, retention, authorization, privacy/delete policy, schema evolution and replay correctness still have to be designed.\n\nChoose it where history, reconstruction or temporal queries are a real product invariant, such as a ledger-like domain. Do not event-source ordinary CRUD merely because a broker is available.\n\nDistinguish internal domain events from public integration events so a private aggregate refactor does not silently break consumers; the modeling boundary is covered in (24-domain-driven-design.tactical-modelling-it.q3).","difficulty":"extra"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q9","q":"Your consumer group rebalances every few minutes and reprocesses messages. Which settings cause that, and how do you stop it?","a":"Kafka liveness has two different clocks:\n\n- the heartbeat thread answers session.timeout.ms\n- the application poll loop must return within max.poll.interval.ms\n\nA handler can keep heartbeating while processing a batch, yet still be removed when the poll interval expires. Uncommitted offsets then move with the partition and records are delivered again.\n\n| Setting | Controls | First response |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| max.poll.records | records returned per poll | lower it when batch work is slow |\n| max.poll.interval.ms | maximum time between polls | raise only when work is genuinely bounded and long |\n| session.timeout.ms / heartbeat.interval.ms | member liveness | tune for the network and client version |\n| fetch.min.bytes / fetch.max.wait.ms | fetch batching versus latency | adjust from measured workload |\n| assignment strategy | rebalance behavior | use a supported cooperative/static strategy when its rollout contract fits |\n| group.protocol | which rebalance protocol runs | `consumer` (KIP-848) moves assignment to the broker; the client default is still `classic` |\n\n**The usual fix is bounded work:**\n- reduce the batch\n- move slow work off the poll thread with an explicit pause/claim model\n- apply downstream backpressure\n\nA larger timeout alone can hide a stuck consumer. Commit after the protected effect and make redelivery safe; no rebalance setting creates exactly-once business effects.\n\n**Kafka 4.x changed the protocol, not just its settings.** The classic protocol rebalances behind a group-wide synchronization barrier: one member that joins, leaves or stalls disturbs every member, and cooperative assignment only shortens the pause.\n\nKIP-848, generally available since Kafka 4.0, moves assignment to the broker's group coordinator and reconciles members incrementally, so a member whose assignment does not change is not interrupted.\n\nA client opts in with `group.protocol=consumer`. `session.timeout.ms` and `heartbeat.interval.ms` are then not supported — the broker's `group.consumer.*` settings own liveness — and a server-side assignor (`group.remote.assignor`) replaces the client strategy.\n\n`max.poll.interval.ms` still applies: a consumer that stops polling still loses its partitions.\n\nSee (25-microservice.04-messaging-event-driven-at-scale.q1) and (08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q6).","difficulty":"advanced"},{"id":"08-message-queue.reliability-delivery-semantics.q10","q":"An event is a published contract you cannot recall. How do you design and evolve one without breaking consumers you do not control?","a":"An event can remain in retention and be read by consumers that did not exist when it was published. Design the contract for replay, not only for today's caller.\n\n- publish a fact such as OrderConfirmed separately from a command such as ConfirmOrder\n- choose a payload shape deliberately: enough data for an independent consumer, but no private aggregate structure or undeletable PII\n- key by the entity whose order matters, and state that order is only within a partition or delivery scope\n- give every event a stable identity and schema version; do not use a body-level version field to hide an uncontrolled compatibility break\n\nCompatibility mode is a policy of a particular registry/subject and deployment sequence, not a universal property of all messages.\n\nAdditive fields are commonly safer; removal, rename, enum narrowing and semantic changes need a migration plan. Test old and new consumers against the actual wire format.\n\n**If compatibility cannot be maintained:**\n1. publish a new version/topic\n2. dual-publish for a bounded migration window\n3. move consumers\n4. retire the old contract\n\nA compacted log is a state projection with tombstone and retention semantics, not a substitute for deletion/privacy design. Replay also needs authorization, rate limits and safe effects.\n\nContract-test the fields consumers actually read; the compatibility test boundary is (26-testing-strategy.integration-tests-that-tell-the-truth.q4), and the data-protection implications are in (13-security-oauth2.protecting-the-data-you-store.q3).","difficulty":"advanced"}]}]},"hasVi":true,"stem":"08-message-queue","sourceOwners":{"10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q1":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q2":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q7":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q12":"design-review-framework","11-system-design-cases.the-big-prompts.q21":"design-review-framework","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q3":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q4":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q5":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.framework-building-blocks.q6":"traffic-caching-building-blocks","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q1":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q2":"surviving-high-load","10-system-design-rate-limit.surviving-high-load.q3":"surviving-high